 ### [Neural Radiosity](6755-neural-radiosity.md)
-Neural Radiosity (NR) is an inverse rendering algorithm that represents the radiance function $L(\mathbf{x}, \omega_o)$ as a single neural network $L_\phi(\mathbf{x}, \omega_o)$ [6755-neural-radiosity.md]. It solves the rendering equation by directly optimizing the network parameters to minimize the $L_2$ norm of the residual of the rendering equation [6755-neural-radiosity.md]. Unlike methods that render images and compare them to ground truth, NR minimizes the physical inconsistency of the radiance field itself [6755-neural-radiosity.md]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its self-training approach on the residual rather than on pixel errors, and by its reliance on automatic differentiation frameworks (like Mitsuba2) to compute gradients of the residual with respect to scene parameters [6755-neural-radiosity.md].
+Neural Radiosity (NR) is an inverse rendering method that solves the rendering equation by representing the radiance field as a neural network and optimizing its parameters to minimize the residual of the equation [6755-neural-radiosity.md]. Unlike standard differentiable rendering which backpropagates through a Monte Carlo graph, NR formulates the problem as a self-training optimization of the radiance function's residual, enabling gradient-based inverse rendering without storing the full computation graph of the renderer [6755-neural-radiosity.md]. It differs from its siblings by directly approximating the solution to the integral equation rather than differentiating a discrete rasterization or path-tracing kernel [6755-neural-radiosity.md].
 
 ### [NeRV (Neural Reflectance and Visibility Fields)](10246-nerv-neural-reflectance-and-visibility-fields.md)
-A method that takes as input a set of images of a scene illuminated by unconstrained known lighting, and produces as output a 3D representation that can be rendered from novel viewpoints under arbitrary lighting conditions, including indirect illumination effects.
+NeRV is a differentiable inverse rendering method that reconstructs a 3D scene representation from images under unconstrained, known lighting, enabling view synthesis and relighting with indirect illumination [10246-nerv-neural-reflectance-and-visibility-fields.md]. It extends the standard emission-absorption volume rendering model by representing the scene as a field of oriented particles that absorb and reflect light, parameterized by neural networks [10246-nerv-neural-reflectance-and-visibility-fields.md]. Unlike its sibling [NeRF](44-radiance-field-optimization.md) which only models emission, or [Neural Radiance Fields](44-radiance-field-optimization.md) which is limited to single point lights, NeRV supports arbitrary environment lighting and one-bounce global illumination by introducing a **Neural Visibility Field** [10246-nerv-neural-reflectance-and-visibility-fields.md]. This field approximates visibility and surface intersection depths via an MLP, reducing the computational complexity of light transport simulation from quadratic/cubic to linear/double-spherical, making optimization tractable for complex scenes [10246-nerv-neural-reflectance-and-visibility-fields.md].
 
